Short:        rsync client with delta transfer
Author:       Claude (Anthropic AI), directed by Paul Hill
Uploader:     Paul Hill
Type:         comm/tcp
Version:      @VERSION@
Architecture: m68k-amigaos >= 3.1
Requires:     68020+, bsdsocket.library (Roadshow, AmiTCP, Miami)

Written by an AI
----------------
The program, its tests and this documentation were written by Claude, an
AI model made by Anthropic, working in Claude Code. Paul Hill set the goals,
chose the features, tested it on the Amiga and reported problems; the code
itself is the AI's. It's tested against the real rsync (about 200
integration tests comparing output, exit codes and resulting files, and
1,600 unit tests run natively and as 68k code), but treat it as you would
any young program: try it on copies of your data first, and use -n (dry
run) before --delete.

What it does
------------
arsync copies files from and to rsync daemons (rsync 3.0 or newer) using
rsync's own protocol, version 32, including delta transfer: a big file with
small changes is updated by sending only the changes, in either direction.

  arsync rsync://host/                      list modules
  arsync rsync://host/module/dir            list files
  arsync -rtv rsync://host/module/dir Work:Dir
  arsync -t host::module/file RAM:file
  arsync -rtv Work:Projects/ rsync://host/module/backup

A trailing / on the source means "the contents of this directory", and a
volume name (Apps:) means its contents too. So a whole volume is backed up
and restored like this; without the / on the restore it would create an
Apps directory inside Apps:

  arsync -rtv --delete Apps: rsync://host/module/Apps
  arsync -rtv --delete rsync://host/module/Apps/ Apps:

Features
  - pull and push, recursive (-r), modification times (-t), dry run (-n),
    progress, verbose output and transfer summary (-v), --stats
  - delta transfer (MD5 block checksums), or whole files with -W
  - --partial (-P): an interrupted transfer keeps what arrived, and the
    next run only fetches the rest
  - choosing what to copy: -u (don't replace newer files), --existing,
    --ignore-existing, --size-only, -c (compare by checksum)
  - -i: rsync's itemized change list (>f.st...... name)
  - compression (-z, zlib and zlibx); packed files (.lha, .lzx, .dms,
    .zip...) are sent as they are
  - --exclude, --include, --exclude-from, --include-from, --filter,
    with rsync's pattern rules
  - --delete and --delete-excluded, in both directions
  - password-protected modules (--password-file, RSYNC_PASSWORD, or prompt)
  - output, options and exit codes like rsync's, so scripts carry over
  - summer time: with a POSIX rule in ENV:TZ (for the UK,
    SetEnv TZ GMT0BST,M3.5.0/1,M10.5.0, then Copy ENV:TZ ENVARC:) each file's time is converted
    with the offset for its own date; otherwise ENV:TZONE (as used by
    SNTP), then the Locale time zone
  - files AmigaDOS can't hold (':' in names, names too long for the
    volume, names differing only in case, files of 2 GB or more) are
    skipped with a warning, never renamed
  - times converted to local time with the Locale time zone
  - Ctrl-C works at any point and leaves no half-written files
  - runs on its own 64 KB stack, so the Shell stack size doesn't matter

Not supported: symlinks, permissions, ssh.

Installation: copy arsync to C: (or anywhere in your path).

History
-------
0.4.3 (2026-10-26)
  - Fixed: a file or directory dated in the hour repeated when summer
    time ends (01:00-02:00 on the last Sunday of October in the UK) was
    copied again on every run. AmigaDOS keeps local time, which can't
    tell the two 01:xx hours apart, so it read back an hour early; arsync
    now compares against the time the Amiga can actually store.

0.4.2 (2026-10-14)
  - Fixed: with -z, a file could fail verification twice ("update
    discarded") when updating an existing copy. GCC miscompiles zlib's
    inflate_fast() for a repeated run that starts in earlier data, so a
    stretch of the new file came out wrong. zlib's inffast.c is now built
    with less optimisation, and a unit test covers the case.

0.4.1 (2026-10-14)
  - Fixed: file times were an hour out for files dated in summer time,
    since OS 3.x's Locale offset has no daylight saving. arsync now reads
    a POSIX rule from ENV:TZ (e.g. GMT0BST,M3.5.0/1,M10.5.0) and uses the
    offset for each file's own date; without it, ENV:TZONE (as used by
    SNTP), then Locale.
  - Readme: a backup and restore example, showing the trailing / needed
    on the restore source.

0.4.0 (2026-10-13)
  - New: --partial, and -P now means --partial --progress as in rsync: a
    transfer cut short (Ctrl-C, lost connection) keeps what arrived, and
    the next run uses it, so only the rest is fetched.
  - New: -u/--update, --existing, --ignore-existing, --size-only and
    -c/--checksum, for choosing what gets transferred.
  - New: -i/--itemize-changes (and -ii), rsync's per-file change codes.

0.3.2 (2026-10-13)
  - New: --stats, rsync's transfer statistics (files, created, deleted,
    literal and matched data, bytes).

0.3.1 (2026-10-13)
  - Fixed: pushing with --delete to some servers stopped at the end with
    "protocol error: unexpected index -3 in goodbye" (the server's
    deletion counts weren't expected). The transfer itself had finished.

0.3.0 (2026-10-13)
  - New: --exclude, --include, --exclude-from, --include-from, --filter.
  - New: --delete and --delete-excluded, pulling and pushing.
  - New: -z compression (zlib and zlibx), --compress-level, --zc.
  - Built with zlib 1.3.1 from source: the compiler's prebuilt zlib is
    miscompiled for 68k and corrupted compressed data.

0.2.0 (2026-10-12)
  - New: push (send local files to a daemon), with delta transfer.
  - Faster MD5/MD4 and rolling checksums.

0.1.0 (2026-10-12)
  - First release: pull from rsync daemons, delta transfer, AmigaDOS
    name and size limits, --progress, Ctrl-C.

